<v Daniel>They summarize the PDFs. They pull the telemetry data. They wrote the incident reports. That is how they built their own mental muscle memory to eventually become senior experts. Right. If the AI is now doing the grunt work and companies are drastically reducing</v>

151
00:18:24.844 --> 00:18:29.648
<v Daniel>their junior roles, how does a 22-year-old graduate ever become a senior expert?</v>

152
00:18:30.069 --> 00:18:41.907
<v Maya>That is the flashing warning light for the future of work. The falling share of junior and non-college jobs at these frontier firms is a direct threat to early career workers, adult learners and first generation students.</v>

153
00:18:42.187 --> 00:18:45.688
<v Daniel>Which is exactly the student body that many public university systems serve.</v>

154
00:18:45.728 --> 00:18:58.471
<v Maya>Yes. The traditional apprenticeship model of entering the workforce is breaking down. Universities can no longer just teach students how to do the baseline tasks. They have to teach students how to be editors, auditors and system builders from day one.</v>

